[2026-01-24]
TITLE: Canvas polygons were connected across paths
MISTAKE:
Used a single canvas path or lineTo() without moveTo(), causing polygons to connect.
CORRECT RULE:
Every polygon must use its own beginPath(), moveTo() for the first vertex, and closePath() before fill/stroke.

[2026-01-24]
TITLE: Treating municipality outline union as requiring >=2 polygons
MISTAKE:
Assumed that a municipality outline must have at least 2 polygons to form a valid union, causing single-polygon municipalities to be rejected or mishandled.
CORRECT RULE:
A municipality outline can be a single polygon. Union operations should handle both single and multiple polygon cases correctly.

[2026-01-24]
TITLE: Failing to emit GeoJSON when zero features exist
MISTAKE:
Code paths that skip writing GeoJSON output when the feature count is zero, causing downstream tools to fail or expect files that don't exist.
CORRECT RULE:
Always emit valid GeoJSON output even if it contains an empty features array. Empty GeoJSON is valid and should be written to maintain consistent output structure.

[2026-01-24]
TITLE: Assuming Excel aggregate (∑) rows are settlements
MISTAKE:
Treated rows containing the "∑" symbol as valid settlement entities, causing aggregate totals to be included as actual settlements in the census data.
CORRECT RULE:
Any row containing the "∑" symbol in any cell must be excluded from settlement-level data. Aggregate rows are for validation only and must never become settlement entities.

[2026-01-24]
TITLE: Allowing duplicate settlement_id to pass silently
MISTAKE:
Failed to detect or handle duplicate settlement_id values, allowing them to pass through the pipeline without remapping or error reporting.
CORRECT RULE:
All settlement_id values must be globally unique. When duplicates are detected, generate deterministic remapped IDs and record the remapping in the issues report. Never allow duplicate IDs to persist.

[2026-01-24]
TITLE: Municipality name detection picking up UI text
MISTAKE:
Municipality name detection logic picked up "Pretraži:" (Search:) text from Excel cells, causing all municipalities to normalize to the same name and get duplicate municipality IDs.
CORRECT RULE:
When detecting municipality names from Excel cells, skip cells containing UI text like "Pretraži", "Search", or cells ending with ":". Prefer using sheet names as fallback when name detection fails.
[2026-01-24]
TITLE: SVG pack is municipality-level, requires order-based join
MISTAKE:
Assumed settlement-level SVG files with IDs, got municipality-level Raphael.js with no SIDs
CORRECT RULE:
When SVG pack lacks settlement identifiers, use strict order-based join with count equality per municipality, and produce reports and overrides

[2026-01-24]
TITLE: Polygon union is unreliable, use convex hull fallback for outlines
MISTAKE:
Assumed union would work for municipality outlines, union failed
CORRECT RULE:
Always handle 1-polygon case, attempt union only if stable, otherwise use deterministic convex hull and report counts

[2026-01-24]
TITLE: Municipality has SVG but zero valid geometry
MISTAKE:
Assumed svg_path presence implies polygon validity; entire municipalities can fail due to open/stroke paths or transforms. Municipality: Višegrad (1015698) has 163 settlements with SVG but 0 valid geometry.
CORRECT RULE:
Track drop reasons per municipality, keep scale-aware closure, and require forensic report before claiming geometry coverage.

[2026-01-24]
TITLE: Using GIS validity as a hard gate drops too much geometry
MISTAKE:
Rejected settlement polygons solely because Turf booleanValid failed, even when they are usable for rendering/triangulation.
CORRECT RULE:
Primary gate is render-valid (finite, non-zero area, non-self-intersecting/triangulatable). GIS-valid is diagnostic only. Use deterministic convex hull salvage when needed.

[2026-01-24]
TITLE: Convex hull salvage can distort settlement shapes
MISTAKE:
Used convex hull salvage as a general fix, which can inflate concave or linear shapes into large blobs and misrepresent geography.
CORRECT RULE:
Measure hull inflation, prefer simplified-ring salvage when possible, and flag high-inflation hulls in metadata and overlays.

[2026-01-24]
TITLE: Assuming polygon IDs match Excel IDs
MISTAKE:
Joined settlement geometry to census/meta by raw sid even though SVG polygon ids and Excel settlement ids use different schemes, producing silent mismatches.
CORRECT RULE:
Treat SVG ids as opaque geometry handles (sid_html). Build an explicit deterministic crosswalk from (mid + normalized_name) with 1:1 matches only, then re-key geometry to canonical Excel sid.

[2026-01-24]
TITLE: Forcing 1:1 settlement polygon identity
MISTAKE:
Assumed settlement polygons must map 1:1 to Excel census settlements and attempted to re-key polygon ids to settlement ids, despite incompatible id and naming universes.
CORRECT RULE:
Treat SVG-derived polygons as territorial micro-areas (poly_id) separate from settlement entities (sid_excel). Polygons may only be linked to municipalities (mid) and used for visualization/territorial substrate. Settlements remain point+graph entities.

[2026-01-25]
TITLE: Municipality outline derivation reported all municipalities missing
MISTAKE:
Report logic produced missing list equal to expected even when municipalities were emitted; misleading diagnostics due to ID scheme mismatch (comparing mid from settlements_meta.csv with mun_code from polygons).
CORRECT RULE:
Missing list must be expected_minus_emitted (same ID scheme), and counts must be internally consistent. When using mun_code scheme, cannot compare to expected municipalities from settlements_meta.csv (which uses mid).

[2026-01-25]
TITLE: Muni-from-fabric union path was broken, forcing fallback for all municipalities
MISTAKE:
All unions failed due to a broken union/buffer-fix path (e.g., 'Must have at least 2 geometries'), causing outputs to be MultiPolygon collections that render like the settlement fabric.
CORRECT RULE:
Use a proper deterministic polygon union implementation; only fallback when union genuinely fails for a specific municipality; report expected/emitted/missing consistently.

